<<~ ahu #/leans>>

!! Leans ~ four stand open, six closed with the ruling

''The ruling of 2026-09-12 (#/the-lifecycle) closed six of these and part-closed a seventh.'' Each closed lean keeps its argument and carries the verdict, so a reader meets WHY it closed rather than a shorter list.

# ''The `folded` stage may want to live in `status-why` rather than in `status`.'' A fourth value adds a vocabulary the house must teach every reader and every future gate, where the sentence "folded to `lar:///…`, welded, nothing points here" already carries everything a reader needs and rides a key that exists. Against that: a value gets checked and a sentence gets read, and the whole point of naming the stage is that a gate could refuse to burn anything not standing in it. ''CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — the stage rides neither — it rides `tags`, so a gate checks it AND a filter renders it, which is the property both halves of this lean were reaching for.
# ''The KIND axis may belong in `tags` rather than in a new key.'' Every carrier already carries `tags = ["api/pono/meme"]`, and a `kind/rite` tag would ride a mechanism the house runs. Against: `tags` currently name what LAW binds a carrier, never what kind the carrier holds, and overloading it would make a filter over tags answer two questions at once. ''CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — it does, as `kind/rite`. The prefixes keep the two questions apart on one key, so a filter never guesses which one it is answering.
# ''The `ahi-kā`-over-a-carrier reading stands unbuilt and possibly unbuildable at this grain.'' The ratio wants a baseline rate — "the realm's own historical rolls per unit of that yardstick" — and a carrier's own history runs far too short and too bursty for one (a carrier touched in three commits on one day and never again reads identically to a carrier touched three times across a year). The estimator may want the ''bag'' as its unit and a carrier as an observation, which changes the design. `-> ?`
# ''A batched sitting and the mass-delete brake may fight.'' The brake suspends a wave above a quarter of a bag's carriers because a flood signals a mistake. A deliberate seasonal sitting floods ''on purpose''. Whether the brake reads as a safety the sitting must clear, or as an instrument that simply does not apply to an operator-called rite, stands unruled. ''CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — they never meet. The brake governs a sync wave writing tombstones into a live group; the sitting is a READING that writes nothing and burns nothing. The operator calls the fire by hand afterwards.
# ''Zero-inbound from `meme check --edges` overstates its own reach'' — it reads `.mem` text alone and never source comments, the CHANGELOG, skill files, or memory. A weld that trusts it alone will strand exactly the references that cost most to find. Whether the weld step grows a source-tree grep, or whether the edge reader learns to see `lar:` URIs in TypeScript comments, stands open. `-> ?`
# ''`lares-history` reads as an archive and does not act as one.'' Its contents arrived by rename, one has since burned out of it, its addresses sit outside `ha.ka.ba/`, and 22 dangling edges name into it. Either it becomes an archive properly or it stops being named as one. ''CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — it stopped being named as one. The operator ruled it a HARVEST ROOM, the rite walked it end to end, and git carries the archive role alone — #/the-fire records the walk.
# ''`retain` carries no office.'' It rode one once (`cc3f85022`) and no code reads it. Whether it becomes the disposition instruction NARA's schedules describe — attached at pour, read at the sitting — or retires as a field, stands open. ''CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — it retires. The disposition rides the `lifecycle/*` tag, and 657 carriers dropped the key in one sweep — a key retired on 59 carriers while riding 600 others is a warning nobody can act on.
# ''Nothing renders `status` or `status-why`.'' A reader inside the wiki sees neither. A lifecycle whose stages reach only the reader of raw `.mem` bytes governs a smaller house than it thinks. Whether a stage ought to render, and where, stands entirely undesigned here. ''PART-CLOSED 2026-09-12'' — the STAGE renders now, because `tags` is a mechanism TiddlyWiki indexes and `[tag[lifecycle/designed]]` answers. The witness SENTENCE still reaches the reader of raw bytes alone, and where `status-why` ought to surface stands undesigned.
# ''The promotion receipt remains the declared blocker'' (`canon-boundary.mem:97`), and this design leans on promotion for its `docs/pono` → `api/pono` transition. So the rite this carrier describes for its own crossing cannot yet be recorded the way the boundary says it must. `-> ?`
# ''The field never answered who may burn a thing they did not receive.'' The scout could reach no shrine or Jinja Honchō source on it, and this carrier therefore builds its who-may-burn rule on the house's own witness law (`hoike.mem:77`) rather than on a borrowed one. If the rule reads wrong, the house's own reasoning carries the fault and no borrowed authority softens it. `-> ?`

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>

!! Questions ~ three ruled, four standing

''RULED 2026-09-12.'' Q1 — the key SPLITS, and further than either option asked: the stage rides `tags` as `lifecycle/*` and the kind rides `tags` as `kind/*`, so both checkable rules Option B wanted are statable and neither needs a key of its own. Q5 — `lares-history` STOPS being named an archive; it became the harvest room, and the rite emptied and deleted it. Q6 — `retain` RETIRES; `cacheable` stays, as an instruction to the API server behind the Lares that no house code reads.

''The four below still stand at the operator's hand.''

# ''Does any class of carrier MUST-carry a `status`?'' 669 of 726 carry none. A rule reading "every carrier under `docs/pono` and `api/pono` carries one" would cost a sweep across 265 carriers and would make the ladder mean something; leaving it optional keeps the shelf cheap and keeps the ladder advisory. Which?
# ''Who calls the fire, and does an authored ending skip the call?'' This carrier proposes: the operator calls, any hand carries, and a disposition ruled at pour time (`retain = false`, or its successor) stands as a call already made. Does the operator hold that, or does every burn want a fresh call at the moment?
# ''What schedules the sitting?'' This carrier proposes: an ahi-kā-shaped reading names candidates and never burns; the operator calls a Koshōgatsu-shaped season at a phase boundary. Does the reading get built at all, or do candidates simply surface when a spirit closes the last lean on a floor?
# ''Does this carrier cross to `api/pono/otakiage`, and if so does it absorb `api/pono/meme#/retirement` or stand beside it?'' The existing law governs the NAME (deprecate-never-redefine); this design governs the BYTES. One carrier holding both would make the whole discipline readable in one place; two would keep each law's scope legible. Which?

<<~/ahu>>

